# Locks — `synchronized` and `ReentrantLock`

## `synchronized` — the built-in lock

**Mutual exclusion** — at most one thread in the guarded code. Every object carries a hidden **monitor**; the keyword acquires it on entry, releases on exit (even on exception).

```java
class Counter {
    int count = 0;
    synchronized void increment() { count++; }
    synchronized int get() { return count; }   // reads lock too
}
```

## A lock guarantees two things — same lock on **every** access, reads included

- **Atomicity** — read-modify-write uninterrupted · **Visibility** — release flushes writes, acquire reads latest (happens-before)

## `ReentrantLock` — explicit; adds `tryLock`, timeouts, `lockInterruptibly`, fairness, `Condition`s

```java
lock.lock();
try   { balance -= amount; }
finally { lock.unlock(); }   // always
```

## Watch out — **reentrant** (re-acquiring a held lock is fine), but **deadlock**: acquire locks in a **consistent order**, keep sections **short**
